            "text": "At dawn, the city seems to hold its breath. Windows catch a pale silver, traffic lights blink uncertainly, and somewhere a bakery opens its door to the cold street. For a few minutes, before errands and obligations gather momentum, the world feels unfinished. This is when small things become noticeable: a sparrow balancing on a wire, steam rising from a sewer grate, the soft click of someone locking a bicycle.\n\nI like that hour because it offers no grand revelations. It does not promise transformation or demand gratitude. It simply makes room for attention. In daylight, we are trained to hurry past ordinary details, treating them as background. But the background is where life actually happens. A hand warming around a cup. A message written and deleted. A dog tugging toward a familiar tree. These moments are not spectacular, yet they anchor us more reliably than spectacle ever could.\n\nPerhaps attention is a quiet form of love. To notice something is to grant it temporary citizenship in the mind. We cannot keep every morning, conversation, or face, but we can meet them fully while they remain. Later, memory will rearrange the scene, polishing some edges and darkening others. That is not failure. It is the mind's way of carrying light forward.\n\nSo tomorrow, if the morning arrives quietly, I hope to resist the instinct to fill it immediately. I will listen for the bakery door, watch the sparrow, and let the unfinished world remain unfinished for a little while longer. In that pause, nothing dramatic needs to happen. Existing, observing, and breathing may be enough."
          }
